{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":193570,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"262ede78-b0ab-48dc-b168-d58dfece44d4","total_cost_usd":0.7119685,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32127,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":14560,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32127,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":14560,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32127},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":18105,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.018205,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":14560,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":32127,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.6937635,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"researcher\",\n  \"planet\": {\n    \"radius_m\": 200000,\n    \"surface_gravity_ms2\": 4.0,\n    \"atmosphere_top_m\": 72000,\n    \"scale_height_m\": 5500,\n    \"sea_level_density_kgm3\": 0.6\n  },\n  \"bands\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"suborbital\",\n      \"altitude_min_m\": 80000,\n      \"altitude_max_m\": 120000,\n      \"orbital_speed_ms\": 730,\n      \"period_s\": 2581\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"low\",\n      \"altitude_min_m\": 140000,\n      \"altitude_max_m\": 220000,\n      \"orbital_speed_ms\": 649,\n      \"period_s\": 3680\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"high\",\n      \"altitude_min_m\": 250000,\n      \"altitude_max_m\": 340000,\n      \"orbital_speed_ms\": 569,\n      \"period_s\": 5470\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reentry\": {\n    \"heating_onset_speed_ms\": 350,\n    \"plasma_onset_speed_ms\": 600,\n    \"heating_velocity_exponent\": 3.0,\n    \"drag_coefficient_hull\": 0.35,\n    \"drag_coefficient_shield\": 1.5,\n    \"reference_area_m2\": 3.5\n  },\n  \"derivation\": [\n    \"Planet first: R = 200000 m (1/31.9 of Earth's 6371 km radius, inside the charter's 160-320 km window) and g0 = 4.0 m/s2 (between Mars 3.71 and a notional heavy rock). Everything below is derived from these two numbers only.\",\n    \"Gravitational parameter: mu = g0 * R^2 = 4.0 * (2.0e5)^2 = 4.0 * 4.0e10 = 1.6e11 m^3/s^2. Surface escape speed = sqrt(2*mu/R) = sqrt(2*1.6e11/2.0e5) = sqrt(1.6e6) = 1265 m/s, so the whole game lives inside a ~1.3 km/s velocity envelope.\",\n    \"Circular speed, suborbital band midpoint h = 100000 m: v = sqrt(g0*R^2/(R+h)) = sqrt(1.6e11 / 3.0e5) = sqrt(533333) = 730.3 m/s. Period T = 2*pi*(R+h)/v = 2*pi*300000/730.3 = 1884956/730.3 = 2581 s = 43.0 min.\",\n    \"Same substitution, low band midpoint h = 180000: v = sqrt(1.6e11/3.8e5) = sqrt(421053) = 648.9 m/s, T = 2387610/648.9 = 3680 s = 61.3 min. High band midpoint h = 295000: v = sqrt(1.6e11/4.95e5) = sqrt(323232) = 568.5 m/s, T = 3110177/568.5 = 5470 s = 91.2 min. ON THE RECORD: the shipped time warp is mandatory. A three-pass aerobrake from the high band spans roughly 4.5 hours of simulated time; at 1x that is unplayable, and no choice of R in the charter's window fixes it (T scales as (R+h)^1.5 / (sqrt(g0)*R)).\",\n    \"Atmosphere: rho = rho0 * exp(-h/H) with rho0 = 0.60 kg/m3 (half Earth sea level, so the first bite of air is forgiving) and H = 5500 m. Sensible top taken where rho falls to 1e-6 kg/m3: h = -H * ln(1e-6/0.6) = -5500 * ln(1.667e-6) = -5500 * (-13.30) = 73150 m, rounded down to atmosphere_top_m = 72000 m. That is 8000 m clear of the lowest band floor (80000 m), so nothing in a band orbit feels drag - aerobraking is a deliberate dip, exactly as 2.3.1 requires.\",\n    \"Ballistic coefficient check. Frontal area A = 3.5 m2 (a ~2.1 m diameter hull). Staged, empty: beta = m/(Cd*A) = 900/(1.5*3.5) = 900/5.25 = 171 kg/m2. Staged, full hold (2.3.5 says a full hold roughly doubles mass): 1600/5.25 = 305 kg/m2. Both sit inside the real capsule range of 300-500 and well above the 50 kg/m2 cliff where first air contact eats the whole orbit. Unstaged, with a fuelled stage still attached: 2400/(0.35*3.5) = 2400/1.225 = 1959 kg/m2 - an order of magnitude slipperier, which is the physics behind 'shallow braking passes before you stage'.\",\n    \"Delta-v per aerobrake pass, using the standard periapsis-pass approximation dv = v_p * rho_p * L / (2*beta) with effective path length L = sqrt(2*pi*H*r_p). At periapsis altitude 40000 m: r_p = 240000, L = sqrt(2*pi*5500*240000) = sqrt(8.294e9) = 91070 m; rho_p = 0.6*exp(-40000/5500) = 0.6*exp(-7.273) = 0.6*6.94e-4 = 4.16e-4 kg/m3. Staged empty (beta = 171): dv = 764 * 4.16e-4 * 91070 / 342 = 28.95/0.342 -> 84.6 m/s per pass. At periapsis 55000 m the same formula gives rho_p = 0.6*exp(-10.0) = 2.72e-5 and dv = 5.5 m/s - a feather. So the tunable pass depth spans a factor of ~15 in braking authority between 55 km and 40 km, which is the room the Playtester needs to find the 2-4 pass optimum.\",\n    \"Entry speeds at the atmosphere interface (r = 272000 m), coming down from each band on an orbit with periapsis dropped to 40000 m (r_p = 240000). Vis-viva v = sqrt(mu*(2/r - 1/a)), 2/r = 7.3529e-6. Suborbital (r_a = 300000, a = 270000): v = sqrt(1.6e11*(7.3529e-6 - 3.7037e-6)) = sqrt(583872) = 764 m/s. Low (r_a = 380000, a = 310000): sqrt(1.6e11*4.1271e-6) = sqrt(660336) = 813 m/s. High (r_a = 495000, a = 367500): sqrt(1.6e11*4.6318e-6) = sqrt(741088) = 861 m/s.\",\n    \"Heating, Sutton-Graves form q = k * sqrt(rho/Rn) * v^3 with exponent 3.0. Band-to-band ratio at equal periapsis depth: (861/764)^3 = 1.127^3 = 1.43, and (813/764)^3 = 1.20. So each band up is ~20-43% hotter at peak flux before mass effects, and the high band's slower, longer pass integrates more total heat still. The velocity-cubed law is what makes that spread superlinear rather than proportional.\",\n    \"Heating and plasma thresholds. Heating onset 350 m/s = 0.48 of suborbital circular speed, which is the GDD's 'roughly half of orbital speed' read literally. Plasma onset 600 m/s = 0.82 of that same circular speed, chosen above heating onset so plasma is a strictly narrower, later cue: a high-band entry at 861 m/s glows from interface, and the chute window opens only after drag has removed ~260 m/s.\",\n    \"Absolute flux sanity check (this is where game scale departs from reality). At the high-band entry, rho = 4.16e-4, v = 861, nose radius Rn = 1.5 m: q = 1.7415e-4 * sqrt(4.16e-4/1.5) * 861^3 = 1.7415e-4 * 0.01665 * 6.38e8 = 1850 W/m2 = 0.185 W/cm2. Apollo peaked near 500 W/cm2. Nothing ablates at 0.19 W/cm2; see notes - heat must be a normalised game quantity that keeps the v^3*sqrt(rho) SHAPE, not a real watt figure.\",\n    \"Terminal velocity and the parachute, from the same rho0. Staged empty ship on the bare shield at sea level: v_t = sqrt(2*m*g0/(rho0*Cd*A)) = sqrt(2*900*4.0/(0.6*1.5*3.5)) = sqrt(7200/3.15) = sqrt(2286) = 47.8 m/s - lethal, so the chute is not optional. To hit the 5 m/s soft-landing line the chute needs Cd*A = 2*m*g0/(rho0*v^2) = 7200/(0.6*25) = 480 m2, i.e. ~343 m2 of canopy at Cd 1.4, a ~21 m disc. Note v_t scales as sqrt(m): doubling the hold to 1800 kg raises touchdown speed by sqrt(2) = 1.41x under the same canopy, which is precisely the Storage-vs-Parachute pairing asserted in 2.3.6, now with a number on it.\"\n  ],\n  \"sources\": [\n    { \"claim\": \"Convective stagnation-point heating scales as v^3 * sqrt(rho/Rn); coefficient k = 1.7415e-4 in SI units\", \"reference\": \"Sutton-Graves relation (standard entry-aerothermodynamics correlation), quoted from knowledge\" },\n    { \"claim\": \"Exponential atmosphere rho = rho0*exp(-h/H) and H = R_specific*T/g\", \"reference\": \"Isothermal hydrostatic (barometric) atmosphere; Earth's H = 8.5 km at g = 9.81, Mars' H = 11.1 km at g = 3.71\" },\n    { \"claim\": \"Blunt reentry shield Cd near 1.5 and a capsule ballistic coefficient of a few hundred kg/m2\", \"reference\": \"Apollo Command Module: hypersonic Cd ~1.2-1.4, beta ~300 kg/m2\" },\n    { \"claim\": \"Slender launch-configuration body Cd in the 0.3-0.5 range\", \"reference\": \"Standard slender-body/launch-vehicle drag figures\" },\n    { \"claim\": \"A scaled-down planet with a shallow atmosphere capped well below orbit altitude is a workable game abstraction\", \"reference\": \"Kerbal Space Program's Kerbin: R = 600 km, surface g = 9.81 m/s2, atmosphere top 70 km, i.e. Earth gravity on a body far too small to physically produce it\" },\n    { \"claim\": \"Surface gravity chosen in the Mars neighbourhood\", \"reference\": \"Mars surface gravity 3.71 m/s2; Mercury 3.70 m/s2\" }\n  ],\n  \"notes\": [\n    \"The planet is not physically self-consistent, and this is the single largest liberty taken. A 200 km-radius body with g = 4.0 m/s2 implies a bulk density of rho = 3g/(4*pi*G*R) = 12/(4*pi*6.674e-11*2e5) = 12/1.678e-4 = 71500 kg/m3 - about 13x Earth and three times denser than osmium. A realistic 200 km rock would have g near 0.3 m/s2, orbital speeds near 200 m/s, and no atmosphere at all. Gravity is therefore an AUTHORED constant, not a derived one; every other number in this document is derived honestly from it. The charter's air-column requirement and any playable gravity cannot both be satisfied by a real body of this size.\",\n    \"Absolute heat flux is roughly 2500x below real reentry (0.19 W/cm2 vs Apollo's ~500) because entry speeds here are ~860 m/s rather than ~11000 m/s. Ablation, plasma and shield burn-through must be modelled as a normalised 0-1 heat quantity whose RATE follows k*sqrt(rho)*v^3, with k absorbed into the game's own scaling. Do not try to make watts mean anything.\",\n    \"Band-to-band heating spread is compressed relative to the GDD's ambition. Circular speed only falls from 730 to 569 m/s across all three bands, so peak flux from the high band is 1.43x the suborbital figure, not the several-times-hotter the design language implies. If the Playtester wants a steeper return-leg curve, the levers in order of physical honesty are: (1) push the high band further out - it is the (R+h) term doing the work; (2) let higher bands be entered on more eccentric orbits, since entry speed at a fixed periapsis depends on apoapsis; (3) lean on mass, which 2.3.5 already supplies and which raises beta and therefore total integrated heat; (4) only last, raise heating_velocity_exponent toward 3.15, which is still inside the real convective range.\",\n    \"Ballistic coefficients, quoted as the charter asks: 171 kg/m2 staged empty (900 kg dry), 305 kg/m2 staged with a full hold (1600 kg), 1959 kg/m2 unstaged with a fuelled stage (2400 kg). None fall under 100, so the atmosphere brakes the ship rather than stopping it. The dry masses are my assumption, not the GDD's - if the Coder settles on a dry mass below 525 kg the staged beta drops under 100 and the shield configuration starts behaving like a leaf. That is the number to watch.\",\n    \"reference_area_m2 is a single value (3.5 m2) shared by both configurations, with the staging difference carried entirely by Cd (0.35 -> 1.5, a 4.3x jump in braking authority). Physically the staged stack would also be longer and slightly wider; folding that into Cd keeps the config to one area field and one knob per configuration, which suits the single-config-file constraint in 4.4.\",\n    \"Scale height H = 5500 m at g = 4.0 implies R_specific*T = H*g = 22000, so e.g. T = 240 K needs a gas constant of 92 J/kg/K, i.e. a mean molar mass around 90 g/mol - twice CO2, a heavy fluorocarbon-like atmosphere. Defensible for a weird little planet, and worth knowing if anyone writes flavour text about the air. A lighter, Earth-like gas at this gravity would give H near 17 km, which would push the sensible atmosphere top past 200 km and swallow the lower bands.\",\n    \"The plasma-onset speed of 600 m/s is a gameplay cue, not an ionisation calculation - genuine air ionisation needs several km/s and never occurs at this scale. It is placed at 0.82 of suborbital circular speed so the plasma window is a meaningful fraction of the descent. I am also not confident a real parachute survives deployment at 600 m/s; the GDD ties chute safety to plasma clearing rather than to a dynamic-pressure limit, so I have honoured the design rather than the physics here. If it matters later, the honest gate is dynamic pressure q = 0.5*rho*v^2 rather than speed alone.\",\n    \"Band altitudes are given as ranges and orbital_speed_ms/period_s are computed at each band's MIDPOINT. Speed varies across a band's width by about 6% (suborbital) to 8% (high); anything downstream that needs per-altitude speed should recompute from v = sqrt(1.6e11/(200000+h)) rather than interpolating these three numbers.\",\n    \"The 'suborbital' band sits at 80-120 km, above the 72 km atmosphere, so its listed speed is the circular-orbit reference at that altitude rather than a claim about how the player arrives. A genuine suborbital hop reaching 100 km apoapsis arrives far slower than 730 m/s, which is exactly why it is the cheap, forgiving band.\",\n    \"Aerobrake pass depths worth handing the Playtester as a starting grid: periapsis 55 km ~ 5 m/s per pass (feathering, should be uneconomic), 45 km ~ 25 m/s, 40 km ~ 85 m/s, 35 km ~ 250 m/s, below 30 km committed entry. Those come from the same dv formula in the derivation and are the range in which a 2-4 pass optimum, if one exists, has to live.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":118452,"ttft_stream_ms":1406,"time_to_request_ms":238,"type":"result","duration_ms":188875,"uuid":"7541aa86-9076-4361-880f-abb41e36f40e"}
